(* Beginning of Notebook Content *)
Notebook[{Cell["Advanced Lesson 8 - Exercises: Pattern Matching and Conditional Definitions\nMikey Ferguson\nMATH 7013 - Advanced Mathematical Modeling", "Title"], Cell["1: Why pattern matching belongs in functional arguments", "Section"], Cell["Part 1. A definition written without a pattern, f[x] = x^2, attaches to the\nsingle symbol x and to nothing else. It is not a function; it is a stored\nvalue for one particular expression. Writing f[x_] = x^2 attaches to the\npattern \"any one argument\", which is what a function of one variable\nactually means. Pattern matching is how a definition says which calls it\napplies to, and without it a definition applies to exactly one call.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"g", ",", "x", ",", "y"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"g", "[", "x", "]"}], "=", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"g", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"g", "[", "y", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"g", "[", "0", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"g", "[", FractionBox["\[Pi]", "2"], "]"}]}], "}"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 2, an example that fails without pattern matching. Only g[x] returns\nSin[x]. g[y], g[0] and g[Pi/2] all return unevaluated, because none of them\nis the symbol x. The definition is useless for every argument anybody would\nactually want to pass.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"g", ",", "x", ",", "y"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"g", "[", "x_", "]"}], "=", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"g", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"g", "[", "y", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"g", "[", "0", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"g", "[", FractionBox["\[Pi]", "2"], "]"}]}], "}"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["With the underscore all four evaluate: Sin[x], Sin[y], 0 and 1.", "Text"], Cell["2: Why delayed assignment belongs in functional definitions", "Section"], Cell["Part 1. Immediate assignment evaluates the right-hand side once, at\ndefinition time, when the pattern variable is still a bare symbol. Any\nright-hand side whose meaning depends on the argument having a value - a\nderivative, an integral, a random draw, a conditional test, a loop - is\ntherefore computed against the wrong thing and frozen. Delayed assignment\nstores the right-hand side unevaluated and re-evaluates it per call, which\nis what a function body is supposed to do.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"h1", ",", "h2", ",", "x"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"h1", "[", "x_", "]"}], "=", RowBox[{"Length", "[", "x", "]"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"h2", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", RowBox[{"Length", "[", "x", "]"}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"h1", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"h2", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["Part 2, an example that fails without delayed assignment. At definition time\nx is a symbol, and the length of a symbol is 0, so h1 stores the constant 0\nand returns 0 for every argument. h2 stores Length[x] unevaluated and\nreturns 3, which is the answer.", "Text"], Cell["3: Squares of integers, cubes of rationals, everything else unchanged", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"f", ",", "x", ",", "values"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_Integer", "]"}], ":=", SuperscriptBox["x", "2"]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_Rational", "]"}], ":=", SuperscriptBox["x", "3"]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", "x"}]}]], "Input"], Cell["??f", "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", FractionBox["3", "5"], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", "3", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", FractionBox["4", "2"], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", "3.1`", "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Input"], Cell["f[3/5] is 27/125, cubed as a rational. f[3] is 9, squared as an integer.\nf[4/2] is 4, not 8: Mathematica evaluates 4/2 to the integer 2 before f ever\nsees it, so the integer rule applies. This is worth noticing - the pattern\ntests the head of the argument after evaluation, not the way it was typed.\nf[3.1] is 3.1 unchanged, because a machine real is neither Integer nor\nRational and falls through to the general rule.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"values", "=", RowBox[{"Table", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "5"}], ",", "5", ",", FractionBox["1", "2"]}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"ListPlot", "[", "values", "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["The table steps in exact halves, so every other entry is a Rational and gets\ncubed while the entries between are Integers and get squared. The ListPlot\nshows the two interleaved families plainly: a parabola through the integer\npoints and a cubic through the half-integer ones, crossing near the origin.", "Text"], Cell["4: NumberQ against NumericQ", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", "vals", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"vals", "=", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"3", ",", FractionBox["3", "4"], ",", "3.1`", ",", RowBox[{"3", "+", RowBox[{"4", " ", "\[ImaginaryI]"}]}], ",", SqrtBox["2"], ",", "\[Pi]", ",", "\[ExponentialE]"}], "}"}]}], ";"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"NumberQ", "/@", "vals"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"NumericQ", "/@", "vals"}]], "Input"], Cell["The difference. NumberQ asks whether the expression already is an explicit\nnumber - Integer, Rational, Real or Complex. NumericQ asks whether it could\nbe evaluated to a number by applying N.\nNumberQ gives True for 3, 3/4, 3.1 and 3 + 4 I, and False for Sqrt[2], Pi\nand E: those three are exact symbolic expressions, not stored numbers.\nNumericQ gives True for all seven.\nFor universal trigonometric evaluation NumericQ is the right choice. A test\nbuilt on NumberQ would refuse Sin[Pi], Sin[Sqrt[2]] and Sin[E] - exactly the\nexact-symbolic arguments one most wants to handle - because it would decide\nthey are not numbers. NumberQ answers a question about storage; NumericQ\nanswers a question about meaning, and meaning is what a mathematical\nfunction should dispatch on.", "Text"], Cell["5: VectorQ against ListQ against MatrixQ", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", "objs", "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"objs", "=", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"3", ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"3", ",", "4"}], "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", "1", "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"3", ",", "4"}], "}"}], "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "2"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"3", ",", "4"}], "}"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"5", ",", "6"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"7", ",", "8"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], ";"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"ListQ", "/@", "objs"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"VectorQ", "/@", "objs"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"MatrixQ", "/@", "objs"}]], "Input"], Cell["The difference. ListQ asks only whether the head is List - it says nothing\nabout shape or depth. VectorQ asks for a list none of whose elements is\nitself a list: a flat list, depth exactly one. MatrixQ asks for a list of\nequal-length lists of non-lists: a rectangular array, depth exactly two.\nSo: 3 fails all three. {1, 2, 3} passes all three except MatrixQ.\n{{1, 2}, {3, 4}} is a List and a Matrix but not a Vector, since its elements\nare lists. The ragged {{{1}, {2}}, {{3, 4}}} is only a List - its two\nelements have different lengths, so it is not rectangular, and it is three\ndeep anyway. The 2 x 2 x 2 array is only a List: rank three is too deep for\nMatrixQ, which insists on exactly two.", "Text"], Cell["6: A sign-switched sine, via Boolean tests", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"f", ",", "x"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}], "/;", RowBox[{"Positive", "[", "x", "]"}]}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "/;", RowBox[{"!", RowBox[{"Positive", "[", "x", "]"}]}]}]}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["The condition operator \"/;\" attaches a test to a definition: the rule only\nfires when the test returns True. Writing the second case as the negation of\nthe first, rather than as Negative[x], is deliberate - it makes the two\nconditions exhaustive, so x = 0 is covered and no argument falls through\nunevaluated.\nThe plot is the absolute-value-like reflection: the negative half of the\nsine curve is flipped up, so the graph is a positive hump on each side of\nthe origin.", "Text"], Cell["7: Exercise 3 again, using conditional evaluation", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"f", ",", "x"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["x", "2"], "/;", RowBox[{"IntegerQ", "[", "x", "]"}]}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["x", "3"], "/;", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Head", "[", "x", "]"}], "===", "Rational"}]}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", "x"}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", FractionBox["3", "5"], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", "3", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", FractionBox["4", "2"], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", "3.1`", "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"ListPlot", "[", RowBox[{"Table", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "5"}], ",", "5", ",", FractionBox["1", "2"]}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Same results as Exercise 3, reached differently. Instead of naming a head\ninside the pattern, each rule matches anything and then guards itself with a\ntest. The two styles are interchangeable here; the pattern-head version is\nfaster, because Mathematica can reject non-matching calls without evaluating\na condition, but the conditional version generalises to tests that no head\ncan express.", "Text"], Cell["8: Exercise 6 again, using conditional evaluation", "Section"], Cell["Exercise 6 was already written with \"/;\", so there is nothing to change. It\nis repeated here in its Boolean-test form for the record; the two exercises\ncoincide.", "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"f", ",", "x"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}], "/;", RowBox[{"x", ">", "0"}]}]}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "/;", RowBox[{"x", "\[LessEqual]", "0"}]}]}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["9: Exercise 3 again, using Which", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"f", ",", "x"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", RowBox[{"Which", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"IntegerQ", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", SuperscriptBox["x", "2"], ",", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Head", "[", "x", "]"}], "===", "Rational"}], ",", SuperscriptBox["x", "3"], ",", "True", ",", "x"}], "]"}]}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", FractionBox["3", "5"], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", "3", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", FractionBox["4", "2"], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", "3.1`", "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"ListPlot", "[", RowBox[{"Table", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"f", "[", "x", "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "5"}], ",", "5", ",", FractionBox["1", "2"]}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["Which takes alternating test-and-result pairs, evaluates the tests in order,\nand returns the result belonging to the first test that is True. The final\nTrue acts as the catch-all. One definition now carries all three cases,\nwhich reads better than three definitions when the cases are genuinely\nalternatives - and worse when they are separate ideas that might later be\nextended independently.", "Text"], Cell["10: Exercise 6 again, using Which", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"f", ",", "x"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x_", "]"}], ":=", RowBox[{"Which", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"x", ">", "0"}], ",", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", "True", ",", RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "x", "]"}]}]}], "]"}]}]}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"f", "[", "x", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["11: Exercise 3 again, as a conditional replacement rule", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"x", ",", "rule3"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"rule3", "=", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"n_Integer", "\[RuleDelayed]", SuperscriptBox["n", "2"]}], ",", RowBox[{"n_Rational", "\[RuleDelayed]", SuperscriptBox["n", "3"]}], ",", RowBox[{"n_", "\[RuleDelayed]", "n"}]}], "}"}]}], ";"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["3", "5"], ",", "3", ",", FractionBox["4", "2"], ",", "3.1`"}], "}"}], "/.", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "rule3"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Replace", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{FractionBox["3", "5"], ",", "3", ",", FractionBox["4", "2"], ",", "3.1`"}], "}"}], ",", "rule3", ",", RowBox[{"{", "1", "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"ListPlot", "[", RowBox[{"Table", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"Replace", "[", RowBox[{"x", ",", "rule3"}], "]"}]}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "5"}], ",", "5", ",", FractionBox["1", "2"]}], "}"}]}], "]"}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["The same dispatch expressed as rules rather than as definitions. Delayed\nrules (\":>\") are required, not optional: with immediate rules the right-hand\nsides n^2 and n^3 would be evaluated once, at the moment the rule list was\nbuilt, when n is still a bare symbol - the same trap as immediate assignment\nin Exercise 2. The rules are tried in order at each position, so the general\ncatch-all must come last.\nNow compare the two applications, because they do not agree. ReplaceAll\nreturns the list unchanged. It works from the top down, and at the very top\nit finds the whole list {3/5, 3, 4/2, 3.1}, which matches the catch-all n_\nand is replaced by itself. ReplaceAll never revisits what it has just\nreplaced, so it never descends to the elements. The catch-all swallowed the\ncontainer.\nReplace with an explicit level specification of {1} applies the rules to the\nelements and not to the list, and returns {27/125, 9, 4, 3.1} as intended.\nThe ListPlot uses Replace on each scalar for the same reason, and reproduces\nthe two interleaved curves from Exercise 3.\nThe general lesson is worth more than the exercise: a rule set containing an\nunguarded catch-all cannot be applied with ReplaceAll to a structure, only\nto an atom. Either restrict the level, or make the catch-all specific enough\nthat the container does not match it.", "Text"], Cell["12: Exercise 6 again, as a conditional replacement rule", "Section"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"Clear", "[", RowBox[{"x", ",", "rule6"}], "]"}], ";", "\[IndentingNewLine]", RowBox[{"rule6", "=", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"n_", "\[RuleDelayed]", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "n", "]"}], "/;", RowBox[{"n", ">", "0"}]}]}], ",", RowBox[{"n_", "\[RuleDelayed]", RowBox[{"-", RowBox[{"Sin", "[", "n", "]"}]}]}]}], "}"}]}], ";"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", "1"}], ",", RowBox[{"-", "0.5`"}], ",", "0", ",", "0.5`", ",", "1"}], "}"}], "/.", "\[VeryThinSpace]", "rule6"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Replace", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"-", "1"}], ",", RowBox[{"-", "0.5`"}], ",", "0", ",", "0.5`", ",", "1"}], "}"}], ",", "rule6", ",", RowBox[{"{", "1", "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Plot", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"Replace", "[", RowBox[{"x", ",", "rule6"}], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"x", ",", RowBox[{"-", "\[Pi]"}], ",", "\[Pi]"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]], "Input"], Cell["A condition can be attached to a replacement rule exactly as it can to a\ndefinition, and it is tested after the pattern matches but before the\nreplacement is made. If the condition fails, the rule is abandoned at that\nposition and the next rule is tried - which is why the unguarded fallback\nsitting second catches everything the first rule declines.\nThe two applications disagree again, and for the same reason as Exercise 11,\nthough here the wrong answer is disguised. ReplaceAll matches the whole list\nagainst the second rule - the first declines, since a list is not greater\nthan zero - and returns -Sin[{-1, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 1}], which listability turns\ninto a list of numbers. It looks like an answer. It is the wrong answer: the\npositive entries have been negated, because the condition was tested once\nagainst the container instead of five times against the elements.\nReplace at level {1} gives the intended result, in which every entry is the\nodd reflection of the sine. The plot uses the same form, and Plot's holding\nof its first argument means the replacement happens afresh at each sample\npoint, with a genuine number in hand for the condition to test.", "Text"]}, WindowSize -> {1100, 850}, StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]
(* End of Notebook Content *)
